#use(std/code)

#section{Code}::

#code{#use(std/code)} provides #code{#code}: a #strong{raw} body — braces
and #code{#} inside are literal, nothing is interpreted. Block vs inline is
decided by the #code{lang:} keyword: #code{#code(lang: "python")} + an
indented block is a highlighted #code{<pre>} block; #code|{#code{…}}| is
inline. Known languages: #code{gdscript}, #code{plain}, #code{python},
#code{racket}.

#code(lang: "plain")
	#code(lang: "python")
		def greet(name): # not a call
			msg = {"hi": name}  ## dict, raw braces
			return msg

	Inline #code{x = 1} stays verbatim too.

Live:

#code(lang: "python")
	def greet(name): # not a call
		msg = {"hi": name}  ## dict, raw braces
		return msg

Inline #code{x = 1} stays verbatim too.

Leading spaces after the structural tabs are preserved verbatim — a raw block
body has no interior structure, so space-indented code (and ASCII art) is
safe:

#code(lang: "gdscript")
	func _ready() -> void:
	    if health > 0:
	        emit_signal("health_changed", 0, health)

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: Raw-ness is total: #code{#} is never a comment or call inside a #code{#code} body, and braces need not balance in the #emph{block} form (an inline body still scans for its closing brace — use a fence for unbalanced inline content).
	#item: A plain, unhighlighted block is #code{#code(lang: "plain")}; an unknown #code{lang:} is an error listing the known ones.
	#item: A raw block is terminated only by dedent (a non-blank line with fewer tabs); blank lines belong to the block.
	#item: Deeper tabs inside the block stay literal tabs — only one structural tab per nesting level is stripped.
